  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user: \"Write a detailed tutorial on implementing a bounded LRU cache in Python using collections.OrderedDict. Include a complete class, explain get and put behavior, discuss edge cases, and include tests. Continue with a worked example and complexity analysis. Be precise and use meaningful prose and code.\"\n\nWe need produce final answer with detailed tutorial. Need be precise. We can include code. Need likely no external libs except pytest. Need cover OrderedDict move_to_end, popitem(last=False), capacity. Complete class LRU Cache. Explain get and put behavior: get updates recency, returns None or sentinel for missing? Edge cases: capacity 0, negative? key absent, default_factory? Overwrite existing key should not change size but update recency. Evict least recently used. Thread safety? We can note not thread-safe unless locks. Equality? maybe key must be hashable. Use __missing__? Maybe implement get/put as specified. Could include optional default. Include tests with unittest or pytest. Worked example with sequence of puts/gets and expected evictions. Complexity O(1). Also discuss OrderedDict features. Need maybe mention Python 3.7 dict order but no move_to_end/popitem, so OrderedDict.\n\nNeed ensure complete class. Let's design robust class.\n\nOptions:\nclass LRUCache:\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        if capacity < 0: raise ValueError\n        self._capacity = capacity\n        self._store = OrderedDict()\n    def __contains__(self, key): return key in self._store\n    def __len__(self): return len(self._store)\n    @property capacity\n    def get(self, key, default=None):\n        if key not in self._store: return default\n        value = self._store.pop(key)\n        self._store[key] = value\n        return value\nAlternative move_to_end(key) after pop? Actually get: if key in store: self._store.move_to_end(key); return self._store[key]. This is O(1). But careful if key not in. Use try/except KeyError? Could be:\n    def get(self, key, default=None):\n        try:\n            self._cache.move_to_end(key)\n            return self._cache[key]\n        except KeyError:\n            return default\nBut move_to_end will raise if missing. For default None okay. But if key exists with value None, return None",
